/** @page krb5security Security Model

    @tableofcontents

    The \c krb5 module is intended for security-sensitive use. It is
    designed to slot into strict TLS, Kerberos, and Active Directory
    deployments without weakening enterprise policies.

    @section krb5sec_secrets Secret Handling

    - The module never logs secrets. Passwords, session keys, ticket bytes,
      and keytab key material are not written to any log, exception message,
      or string returned from the Qore-level API.
    - Credential metadata APIs (@ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Credentials::getInfo()
      and @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5CredentialCache::listCredentials()) expose
      principals, timestamps, flags, and numeric enctype identifiers, but
      never the session key or ticket bytes themselves.
    - Password-based initial credential acquisition is noninteractive by
      default: \c change_password_prompt is disabled unless explicitly
      requested via
      @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5InitialCredentialsOptions::change_password_prompt.

    @section krb5sec_init_creds Initial Credential Acquisition

    Initial credential acquisition through
    @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Context::acquireCredentialsWithPassword() and
    @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Context::acquireCredentialsWithKeytab() supports
    the options enterprise deployments typically require:

    - KDC canonicalization for
      <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows-server/identity/ad-ds/get-started/virtual-dc/active-directory-domain-services-overview">Active
      Directory</a>
      <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/win32/ad/naming-properties">UPN</a>
      logins
    - Windows
      <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/openspecs/windows_protocols/ms-pac/166d8064-c863-41e1-9c23-edaaa5f36962">PAC</a>
      requests
    - <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc6113">FAST</a>
      armor credential cache configuration
    - explicit enctype lists to reject weak ciphers
    - ticket lifetime and renewable lifetime controls
    - explicit input and output credential caches

    These options are carried as a typed
    @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5InitialCredentialsOptions hash.

    @section krb5sec_gss GSSAPI Hardening

    - GSSAPI token inputs are validated before decoding; malformed hex or
      truncated tokens are rejected before reaching the GSSAPI library.
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext defaults to the Kerberos mechanism
      and to a conservative set of request flags
      (\c GSS_C_MUTUAL_FLAG | \c GSS_C_SEQUENCE_FLAG | \c GSS_C_INTEG_FLAG).
    - GSSAPI contexts can be bound to an explicit
      @ref Qore::Krb5::GssCredential instead of relying on the process
      default credential, which avoids surprising credential selection in
      multi-tenant Qorus services.
    - GSSAPI channel bindings can be supplied explicitly — including the
      <a href="https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc5929">RFC 5929</a>
      \c tls-server-end-point application data used by LDAP-over-TLS and
      HTTPS endpoint binding — to defeat TLS-proxy credential relay
      attacks.
    - Completed contexts expose
      @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext::wrap() and
      @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext::unwrap() for SASL/GSSAPI
      integrity and confidentiality layers when enterprise policy requires
      message-level protection.

    @section krb5sec_sandbox Filesystem Sandbox

    File-backed credential caches and keytabs (\c FILE, \c WRFILE, and
    \c DIR backends) honour %Qore filesystem sandboxing: every read, write,
    or create operation passes through
    \c QoreSandboxManagerHelper::checkFilesystemAccess() against the
    backend's underlying path. Non-filesystem backends (\c MEMORY, \c API,
    \c KCM, \c KEYRING, \c MSLSA for caches; \c MEMORY for keytabs) are
    allowed through without a filesystem check. Any backend outside these
    two allowlists is rejected with a \c KRB5-SANDBOX-ERROR exception so
    that new MIT Kerberos backends cannot accidentally become
    sandbox-reachable without review.

    @section krb5sec_domains Functional Domains

    The module declares functional domains so that %Qore program-level
    access control can block features that are not appropriate for a given
    program:

    - @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Context, @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5CredentialCache,
      @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Keytab, and @ref Qore::Krb5::GssCredential carry
      the \c FILESYSTEM domain because they may read or write cache and
      keytab files.
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Context::acquireCredentialsWithPassword(),
      @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Context::acquireCredentialsWithKeytab(), and the
      entire @ref Qore::Krb5::GssClientContext class carry the \c NETWORK
      domain because they contact the KDC.
    - @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Principal and @ref Qore::Krb5::Krb5Credentials
      carry no functional domain because they operate purely on in-memory
      data.

    Programs running with \c PO_NO_NETWORK or \c PO_NO_FILESYSTEM will
    therefore fail to parse code that reaches network or filesystem
    operations, without needing to execute anything.

    @section krb5sec_cancellation Cooperative Cancellation

    Every blocking operation — initial credential acquisition, GSSAPI
    context initialization, and GSSAPI message protection — checks for
    cooperative thread cancellation before calling into the underlying
    library. Long-running authentication attempts in Qorus workflows can
    therefore be interrupted cleanly.

    @section krb5sec_exceptions Exception Reference

    The module raises the following %Qore exceptions. All exception messages
    include the originating operation context and, where applicable, the
    underlying Kerberos or GSSAPI error message. Secrets are never included
    in exception messages.

    - \c KRB5-INIT-ERROR — failure to initialize a Kerberos 5 runtime
      context
    - \c KRB5-REALM-ERROR — failure to retrieve the default realm or a
      principal realm
    - \c KRB5-PRINCIPAL-ERROR — failure to parse, copy, unparse, or
      introspect a principal
    - \c KRB5-CACHE-ERROR — credential-cache resolve, open, initialize,
      store, or enumerate failure
    - \c KRB5-CREDENTIALS-ERROR — credential construction, copy, or
      metadata failure, including hex decoding failure of the session key
      or ticket
    - \c KRB5-KEYTAB-ERROR — keytab resolve, open, lookup, add-entry, or
      enumerate failure
    - \c KRB5-INIT-CREDS-ERROR — initial credential acquisition failure
      (including bad option values, KDC errors, and wrong password)
    - \c KRB5-GSS-ERROR — general GSSAPI failure from
      \c gss_init_sec_context, \c gss_import_name, \c gss_krb5_import_cred,
      \c gss_wrap, \c gss_unwrap, or \c gss_wrap_size_limit
    - \c KRB5-GSS-ARG-ERROR — invalid arguments to a GSSAPI method (for
      example a negative \c qop, an unsupported mechanism, or malformed
      channel-binding options)
    - \c KRB5-GSS-STATE-ERROR — GSSAPI operation attempted on a context
      that is not complete
    - \c KRB5-TOKEN-ERROR — malformed hex input for a token, wrapped
      message, channel-binding field, or key
    - \c KRB5-SANDBOX-ERROR — a credential-cache or keytab backend is not
      on the sandbox allowlist (see @ref krb5sec_sandbox)

    In addition, any filesystem sandbox violation surfaces as the standard
    \c FILESYSTEM-ACCESS-DENIED exception from the %Qore sandbox manager.
*/
